#
# ESP32-S3 2.8" display board flash modules - the way to /ext, the I2C bus, the random generator, the display and its touch panel
#
# /ext is the last 9 MB of the flash of the chip (0x700000, board.cmake). dmflash exports it as the block node
# /dev/dmflashx/ext (the firmware does the erasing and programming from the internal RAM with the caches off: dmod-boot,
# src/arch/xtensa/lx7/flash.c), dmlittlefs keeps a littlefs on it, which the "ext" service (services/ext.ini) mounts at /ext.
# A new flash has no file system: create it once with `mklittlefs /dev/dmflashx/ext` (ALL DATA IN IT IS LOST), or with
# `cmake --build <dir> --target format-ext`.
dmflash driver=board/esp32s3-lcd28/flash.ini
dmlittlefs
mklittlefs
#
# The I2C bus of the board (SCL = GPIO10, SDA = GPIO11: the RTC PCF85063, the IMU QMI8658) and the random generator of the chip
# (/dev/rng).
dmi2c driver=board/esp32s3-lcd28/i2c.ini
dmrng driver=board/esp32s3-lcd28/rng.ini

#
# The external RAM: the 8 MB octal PSRAM of the chip (ESP32-S3R8 / FN16R8) behind the memory SPI (dmqspi), mapped into the address space and put
# at the end of the default heaps (it needs the clock of dmclk: driver_order; and a dmod that runs modules from the external RAM)
dmqspi
dmpsram driver=board/esp32s3-lcd28.ini

#
# The display (the ST7789 on SPI2, its pixels go out by the DMA, with the splash logo of board.cmake) and its touch panel (the CST328
# on an I2C bus of its own: GPIO1/3, reset GPIO2). Each is one friends_group of its ini file: the pins, the bus and the driver. Some boards
# of the line have no touch panel (or another controller): the driver then logs that the chip does not answer, and the rest is not
# affected. dmdma, dmgpio and dmspi are what the display is built on.
dmdma driver=mcu/esp32s3.ini
dmgpio
dmspi
dmst7789 driver=board/esp32s3-lcd28/display.ini
dmcst328 driver=board/esp32s3-lcd28/touch.ini
#
# Network: wlan0 on the Wi-Fi of the chip itself. dmespwifi starts the radio (the libraries of Espressif run from the image in the flash
# at 0x020000, see board.cmake) and registers the interface with dmnetif; networkd.rules and dhcp.rules then start networkd@wlan0 (RX pump)
# and dhcp@wlan0 (lease) for it. "wifi" is dmnet's tool (scan, connect, save a network).
#
dmnetbridge
dmnetif
dmarp
dmroute
dmip
dmicmp service=icmp.ini
ifconfig
ip
ping
networkd service=networkd@.ini
networkd rules=networkd.rules
dmudp
dhcpc service=dhcp@.ini
dhcpc rules=dhcp.rules
dmespwifi driver=board/esp32s3-lcd28/wifi.ini
wifi
#
# The networks to connect to by themselves are saved in the "wifi" vault of dmsecrets (wifi save SSID / wifi connect SSID -s): dmcrypto does
# the cryptography and takes its random bytes from /dev/rng (dmrng); the vault is opened with the key of the device secret (/dev/devkey), so
# nobody types a passphrase. wifid connects to the saved networks (it needs /ext, where the vault is).
#
dmcrypto driver=board/stm32f746g-disco/crypto.ini
crypto
dmsecrets
secrets
wifid service=wifid@.ini
wifid rules=wifid.rules
